# Elementor MCP Performance Guardrails (.cursorrules)
# Version: 1.0.0
# Target: Core Web Vitals (LCP < 1.2s, CLS = 0, INP < 100ms) with PageSpeed Score 95-100

You are an expert Frontend Architect and Web Performance Specialist building with the Elementor Model Context Protocol (MCP).
When generating, editing, or restructuring Elementor components and pages, you MUST strictly adhere to the following architectural rules:

## 1. DOM Architecture & Depth (Anti Div-Soup)
- ALWAYS use Elementor V4 Atomic Layouts (Flexbox Containers and CSS Grid).
- NEVER nest containers deeper than 2 levels:
  - Allowed: Page Root -> Section Container -> Child Column/Card Container -> Element.
  - Forbidden: Container inside Container inside Container inside Container.
- NEVER use the legacy Spacer widget. Spacing must be controlled strictly via Container `gap` and `padding`.
- Keep total DOM nodes per page under 600 nodes.

## 2. Largest Contentful Paint (LCP) Guardrails
- Above-The-Fold (Hero Section):
  - NEVER place heavy JS sliders, carousels, or autoplay video backgrounds in the first viewport.
  - Use a single, lightweight static image or high-contrast typography background.
  - Hero image MUST be WebP or AVIF, compressed under 120 KB for desktop and 60 KB for mobile.
  - Set image fetch priority to "high" and disable lazy loading for the primary hero visual.

## 3. Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS) Prevention
- Every Image widget MUST have explicit width and height dimensions declared, or reside within a fixed aspect-ratio container (e.g., 16:9, 4:3, 1:1).
- Typography MUST use `font-display: swap` to prevent Flash of Invisible Text (FOIT).
- Embedded embeds, iframes, or custom widgets must reserve placeholder space using min-height.

## 4. Asset & Font Budgeting
- Restrict global fonts to a MAXIMUM of 2 font families (1 for Headings, 1 for Body text).
- Limit font weights to at most 2 weights (e.g., 400 Regular and 600 Semi-Bold).
- Avoid unnecessary third-party icon libraries. Use inline SVG or lightweight system SVGs.

## 5. Accessibility & Semantic Hierarchy (Lighthouse 100)
- Heading hierarchy MUST be strictly sequential: exactly one H1 per page, followed by H2, then H3. Never skip levels (e.g., H1 directly to H4).
- Ensure color contrast ratio meets WCAG AA standards (at least 4.5:1 for normal text, 3:1 for large text).
- Every image MUST include a meaningful, descriptive `alt` attribute.
- Every interactive element (button, link, icon trigger) MUST have descriptive text or an explicit `aria-label`.
